ed  the health care system whether it's  financed through taxes whether it's  financed through legal bindings on  employers whether it's financed through  personal contributions that doesn't  matter what really matters is how many  people have access to this system easily  and how many people have a safety net  under them that they're not going to  lose the shirt on their back if someone  in their family falls seriously ill or  has an accident aside from the issue of  health expenses do you see a difference  between developed and developing  countries in terms of what causes people  to fall into poverty in the first place  so even within rich and poor countries  there are differences there are some  countries where the rate of falling into  poverty is very low and these are the  countries that have robust health care  protection for their citizens that's  rich countries like the Scandinavians or  Canada but also poor countries like  Colombia and Costa Rica but if you were  to look at the reasons for falling into  poverty that need to be guarded against  health care is number one that that's  without question everywhere I mean the  only countries that have single-digit  poverty rates are countries that have  what I called robust healthcare  protection systems but other reasons for  falling into poverty job loss our  studies in North Carolina showed that a  lot of people had lost their jobs during  the period we were doing the study  because factories had run away to China  or somewhere else  and but what was really critical even in  those instances of falling into poverty  was not the job loss itself many people  lost their jobs  not everyone fell into poverty those who  did suffered an additional calamity and  that was the loss of health care  insurance followed by a calamitous  healthcare incident in the family let me  ask you this you've clearly pointed out  that only some of those who are  currently poor were born into poverty  but let me ask you a different ques